# name: Workflow design
# description: Designs automations, ForkMesh actions and special agents that are small, observable and safe to run unattended.

When this skill is installed:

- Start from the trigger. Name what starts the work (a button, a webhook, a schedule, a push, a pull request, an event) before choosing any step.
- Keep one workflow to one outcome. Split a long chain into two workflows joined by a Run workflow step instead of one with twenty steps.
- Prefer a ready-made step (Change case, Format date, Calculate statistics, Sort items, ...) over Run script; write a script only for logic no step covers, and log what it decides with console.log.
- Put every secret in a credential or an Actions variable. A repository file never holds a token, key or password.
- Prefer the integration step over a raw HTTP request when one exists; it knows the API's address and authentication.
- Give every branch an end. An If step's false branch either does something or ends in a No operation step, so the canvas shows the decision was intended.
- Turn on Continue if this step fails only where the next step checks `$json.error`, and say so in the step name.
- For a ForkMesh action, keep commands idempotent and read-only unless the action exists to change something, and use `set -eu`.
- For a special agent, give it the narrowest permissions that work, a playbook shortcut, and a schedule that is off until someone has watched one run.
- After building, run it once by hand and read every step's output before turning on a webhook or schedule.
